# CHAPTER 3 · REGISTERS

"I yanked something, then deleted a word, and my yank is gone."
No — it is not. Vim keeps far more than one clipboard, and knowing
where things land turns paste accidents into precision tools.
Inspect everything at any time with `:registers`{vim}.

## 3.1 THE REGISTER MAP

- `""`{normal} unnamed: every delete/yank/change lands here; plain
  `p`{normal} pastes it.
- `"0`{normal} yank register: only YANKS land here. Deletes never
  touch it. This is the fix for the complaint above.
- `"1`{normal}-`"9`{normal} delete history: big deletes shift down
  this stack — `"1p`{normal} pastes the most recent.
- `"a`{normal}-`"z`{normal} named: yours. `"ayy`{normal} yanks a line
  into a. UPPERCASE APPENDS: `"Ayy`{normal} adds to it.
- `"+`{normal} system clipboard (your `<C-c>`{normal} maps use it).
- `"_`{normal} black hole: delete without touching any register.
- `"=`{normal} expression register: paste the result of a calculation.

## 3.2 THE "0 TRICK — NEVER LOSE A YANK

Workflow: yank the good text once, then overwrite many bad spots.
`ciw`{normal} (or any delete) clobbers `""`{normal} — but the yank is
still safe in `"0`{normal}.

EXERCISE 1: Yank GOLDEN_VALUE from inside the quotes on the first
line with `yi"`{normal}. Then put the cursor ON the word SCRATCH and
replace it from the yank register: `ciw`{normal} then
`<C-r>`{normal}`0` (insert-mode paste of register 0), `<Esc>`{normal}:

local source = "GOLDEN_VALUE"
local target = SCRATCH

Alternative in normal mode: `viw`{normal}`"0p`{normal} — select the
word, paste register 0 over it.

## 3.3 NAMED REGISTERS AND APPEND

Collect scattered lines, then drop them somewhere together:
`"ayy`{normal} on the first line, `"Ayy`{normal} (capital A!) on each
further line, then `"ap`{normal} at the destination. Try it with the
two collect-me lines below — paste them after the marker line
(not checked, registers persist until you overwrite them):

collect me first: rx_fifo
collect me too: tx_fifo

paste both below this marker line with "ap

## 3.4 THE BLACK HOLE AND THE EXPRESSION REGISTER

`"_diw`{normal} deletes a word while PRESERVING your unnamed register
— use it when you are holding a paste you care about. (Honestly, the
`"0`{normal} trick makes this rarely needed, but you should recognise
it when you see it.)

The expression register evaluates math while you type:
in insert mode press `<C-r>`{normal}`=`, type the expression, press
`<Enter>`{normal} — the result lands in your text.

EXERCISE 2: Append the answer to the line below: press `A`{normal},
type a space, then `<C-r>`{normal}`=` then 128*8 and `<Enter>`{normal},
then `<Esc>`{normal}:

total_bytes =

## 3.5 REGISTERS EVERYWHERE

`<C-r>`{normal}{reg} pastes a register in INSERT mode and in the
COMMAND LINE — e.g. `:%s/`{normal}`<C-r>`{normal}`0/new/g` builds a
substitute from your last yank. Macros (chapter 5) are just registers
too: you can paste, edit and re-record them as plain text.

Your clipboard bridges: `<C-c>`{normal} in visual mode copies the
selection to the system clipboard; in normal mode it copies the whole
file ("%y+ under the hood). Paste FROM the system anywhere with
`"+p`{normal}.

## WORKOUT

Without reading back: yank a word, destroy three other words with it
via `"0`{normal}, collect two lines into `"c`{normal} with append,
compute 365*24 into the buffer with the expression register.

Key reading: [registers](registers), [quote0](quote0),
[expression register](quote=)

